fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1102", description: some(value: "An unsatisfiable `requires Eq[T]` at a CONCRETE carrier must be a LOAD DIAGNOSTIC naming the sort and the missing provision -- today it is an INTERNAL EVALUATOR ERROR, and WI-1098 narrowed the population without changing the message.\n\nMEASURED at HEAD (after WI-1098), three DIFFERENT programs, one identical message. Each is `List.contains(cons(head: v, tail: nil), v)` inside an operation body; each LOADS CLEAN and then answers:\n\n  Err(Internal(\"DeferToRequirement: requirement param `__req_eq` not bound in caller\n   frame (running `anthill.prelude.List.contains`, requires-chain owner\n   `anthill.prelude.List.contains`; frame binds [])\"))\n\n (1) PARAMETRIC FIELD -- `sort Hold { entity hold(p: Pair[A = Float, B = Int64]) }`.\n     WI-1098's documented gap: the classifier resolves a field type to its BASE sort,\n     so it sees `Pair`, not `Pair[A = Float]`, and derives nothing rather than claim a\n     Float lawful. REPAIR the author wants told: write the two provision lines, or wait\n     for conditional derivation.\n (2) DISPATCHED eq, NO PROVISION ON THE CARRIER -- `sort Pebble { entity pebble(n:\n     Int64) }` plus a WITNESS `sort PebbleEq { provides PartialEq[T = Pebble];\n     operation eq(a: Pebble, b: Pebble) -> Bool = true }`. `eq(pebble(1), pebble(2))`\n     DISPATCHES fine; the provision files under the WITNESS (WI-1069: provider and\n     carrier are two questions), so `Pebble` itself has no row to build a dictionary\n     from. REPAIR: `provides PartialEq[T = Pebble]` on Pebble.\n (3) GENUINELY NonEq -- `sort Pt { entity pt(x: Float) }`. `requires Eq[T]` is\n     UNSATISFIABLE here, and no provision line can fix it. This one is not a missing\n     declaration at all: it is a program that should be REFUSED, naming `Pt`, `Float`\n     and `Eq`.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A MESSAGE TWEAK. The right site is the CALL, at LOAD: `Driver.has`\ncalls `contains(l, x)` with `x : Pt`, and both the carrier and the spec are in hand\nthere (the typer's per-call subst). At the eval site (`eval.rs` `start_apply_deferred`,\nthe `find_requirement(...).ok_or_else` arm) the SPEC is reachable -- `op_dict_entries`'\n`RequiresEntry.required_sort` for the slot -- but the CARRIER is not, so an eval-side\nedit can only name half of what the ticket asks and would still fire at run time\ninstead of at load. WI-325's `MissingRequiresForSpecOp` is the neighbouring refusal and\ncovers a different shape (an ABSTRACT spec-op call with no covering `requires`); this is\na CONCRETE carrier whose op-scoped `requires` has no provider.\n\nBLAST RADIUS IS THE WHOLE POINT AND MUST BE MEASURED FIRST. Making this a load error\nturns every program in classes (1)-(3) from \"loads, dies later\" into \"does not load\".\nSix suites already pin the current string (`wi822`, `wi861`, `wi945`, `wi855`, `wi817`,\n`wi1083`, `eval_test`) -- read them before changing it, since several assert the\nmessage as the RECORD of a defect rather than as desired behaviour.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - each of the three programs above is REFUSED AT LOAD with a message naming the\n   CARRIER SORT and the missing/impossible provision, driven, one test per class;\n - CONTROL: WI-1098's derived-Eq programs (`wi1098_derive_eq_total_test`) still load\n   and still ANSWER -- the refusal must not fire where a provision was derived;\n - CONTROL: the six suites pinning the current internal message are re-read and each\n   either updated with a reason or shown to be about a different shape;\n - say at each test site which tests fail when the change is backed out;\n - full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: WI-1098's own scope note (\"Even if this ticket were somehow rejected, that panic\nmust become a diagnostic naming the sort and the missing provision\"), measured after\nWI-1098 landed. Same family as WI-945 / WI-1095."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-14T19:40:53Z"))
